---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { SITE_TITLE, SITE_DESCRIPTION } from '../consts';
import portrait from '../assets/marlon-portrait.jpg';
import climbing from '../assets/marlon-climbing.jpg';
import summit from '../assets/cognition-champions-summit.jpg';
---

<BaseLayout title={SITE_TITLE} description={SITE_DESCRIPTION}>
	<h1>About Me</h1>
	<div class="intro">
		<Image
			class="portrait"
			src={portrait}
			loading="eager"
			width={200}
			height={200}
			alt="Marlon Brandão de Sousa, arms folded and smiling broadly, against a plain blue studio background."
		/>
		<div>
			<p>
				Hello! I'm <strong>Marlon Brandão de Sousa</strong>, a software engineer based in Curitiba,
				Brazil. I have worked in technology since 2004, and for much of that time on the systems that
				move people's money: internet banking, international remittances, and cross-border payments.
			</p>
			<p>
				I am also a blind developer, a conference speaker, and a digital accessibility advocate. Those
				two halves are not separate careers. Building financial software that people trust — and that
				everyone can actually use — is the same problem approached from two directions, and it is the
				work I care most about.
			</p>
		</div>
	</div>

	<p>
		If there is one thread through all of it, it is that I like being a layer down. The framework
		rather than the screen, the harness rather than the feature, the add-on rather than the app — the
		part that makes other people's work possible and rarely gets the credit. I have been working on
		the same question, how technology includes people, since around 2005, and I have changed tools
		constantly without ever changing the question. I also think mainly by explaining: if I cannot
		write something down for someone else, I probably do not understand it yet. And I ask for help
		without embarrassment — from colleagues, from an AI agent, from a stranger at a crossing —
		because refusing help to protect your ego makes you worse, your software worse, and the world
		worse.
	</p>
	<p>
		Away from the keyboard I am a Christian and a husband, and my family comes before the rest of
		this page. I sing, I read, I play the trumpet, and I travel as much as I can manage — enough that
		my wife Atena and I spent several years making
		<a href="https://blindando.com.br" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Blindando</a>, a
		podcast about tourism and having fun from the point of view of us blind people, with episodes
		from Havana to Machu Picchu to Stockholm. Most of what I enjoy comes back to the same thing the
		work does: helping people.
	</p>
	<figure class="personal-photo">
		<Image
			src={climbing}
			width={420}
			height={280}
			alt="Marlon mid-climb on a rock face, in a red helmet taped with his name and dark sunglasses, one hand reaching for the next hold. A second climber and coils of rope are out of focus behind him."
		/>
		<figcaption>Climbing, 2017.</figcaption>
	</figure>

	<h2>Professional Experience</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<strong><a href="https://www.ebanx.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">EBANX</a> — Engineer Specialist (Aug 2024 – present):</strong> One of Latin America's largest fintechs, and the
			cross-border payments infrastructure global merchants use to sell into the region. My work has
			two tracks. The first is research into AI for software engineering — how agents earn a place in
			a production engineering organisation rather than a demo, including extensive use of Cognition
			AI's Devin, which took me to Cognition's Champions Summit in San Francisco in June 2026 as one
			of twenty users invited from around the world. The second is writing the base frameworks other teams build on, so that engineering
			operations are something the platform provides rather than something every team reinvents.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong><a href="https://www.remessaonline.com.br" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Remessa Online</a> — Staff Engineer (Nov 2022 – Jul 2024):</strong> International money transfers, on the platform
			that moves funds across borders for individuals and businesses. Beyond the platform itself, I
			built enablement products for other engineering teams, and advised senior management on
			engineering needs and where the largest improvement opportunities actually were.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong><a href="https://www.ebanx.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">EBANX</a> — Senior Engineer, and Chief Engineer at Compass (Aug 2019 – Oct 2022):</strong> During an earlier spell
			at EBANX I led engineering at Compass, its banking operation in the Turks and Caicos Islands,
			licensed there as Compass Trust Ltd. I set up the entire banking system: accounts, card
			management, statements, the credit card authoriser, back office, customer service, and file
			processing. All of it in Rust, all of it on Kubernetes. At its height the Compass card served
			close to ten thousand people in and from the islands, many of whom did not qualify for a card
			from an established commercial bank.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong><a href="https://www.itau.com.br" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Itaú Unibanco</a> — Internet Banking Platform (Mar 2011 – Jul 2019):</strong> Development and evolution of the
			framework behind the internet banking of Brazil's largest private bank, along with maintenance
			of the internet banking core system and the design of interoperability layers between systems.
			I also advised mobile and internet banking front-end teams on accessibility.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Accenture — Senior Software Developer, then Analyst Programmer (2008–2011):</strong>
			CMMI level 5 audits on ABAP projects, turning audit findings into process improvements, and
			later supervising the audit team — training auditors and building the tooling the
			organisation's quality and engineering processes ran on.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Rede Globo — Software Developer (2004–2008):</strong> Research and development for
			Brazil's largest broadcaster: prototyping and evaluating new technology against the station's
			internal demands, presenting the resulting projects, and helping establish a basic software
			development process.
		</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>Speaking &amp; Community</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<strong>Founder &amp; Editor-in-Chief — BlindTec:</strong> I created and lead
			<a href="https://www.blindtec.com.br" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">BlindTec</a>,
			Brazil's leading portal and podcast for assistive technology, publishing technical articles,
			tutorials, and interviews on screen readers, software accessibility, and tech careers.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Track Coordinator — The Developer's Conference (TDC):</strong> Coordinator of the
			Accessibility track at TDC São Paulo in 2018, and a speaker at TDC and DevDay across several
			years. The talks are listed below.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>EasyWay — second place, FIAP StartupOne:</strong> With a designer and two other
			engineers, I built the prototype of an indoor location system to help people find their way
			through complex venues such as expo centres, shopping malls, and stadiums.
		</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>Talks &amp; Interviews</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<strong>Cognition Champions Summit</strong> — Cognition's headquarters, San Francisco, June
			2026. For its first Champions Summit, Cognition brought together twenty of its top Devin users
			from around the world — teams already running Devin in production on migrations, internal
			tooling and automation — for a day of workshops on the workflows that matter most to each of
			them. I was one of the twenty, and I appear in the
			<a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CHHtsJGivE4" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"
				>summit video</a
			>, at 2:05, on what made the day useful: sessions where the real problems with the product
			were discussed as openly as the solutions we had found for them.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>AI agents in production</strong> — second EBANX Engineering Meetup, Curitiba, November
			2025, co-presented. On the mature and strategic use of AI agents in an
			engineering organisation: what we measured, why it is not another hype cycle, and a live
			build of a tested service in fifteen minutes. The meetup was not recorded, so the talk is
			written up as
			<a
				href="https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/ai-agents-hype-driving-core-business-value-ebanx-brand%C3%A3o-de-sousa-gs94f"
				target="_blank"
				rel="noopener noreferrer"
				>“AI Agents are Not Hype: They are Driving Core Business Value at EBANX”</a
			>.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>“Escolhi uma plataforma para desenvolver. E agora?”</strong> — Accessibility track, TDC
			Florianópolis, 2018.
			<a href="https://thedevconf.com/palestrante/marlon-brandao-de-sousa" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Speaker profile</a>.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>“Programar sem enxergar”</strong> —
			<a href="https://www.hipsters.tech/programar-sem-enxergar-hipsters-82/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Hipsters Ponto Tech #82</a>, February 2018.
			A conversation on whether, and how, you write software without sight.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>“Como cegos desenvolvem software: da concepção à implementação”</strong> — DevDay, Belo
			Horizonte, September 2017.
			<a href="https://www.infoq.com/br/presentations/como-cegos-desenvolvem-software-da-concepcao-a-implementacao" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Video on InfoQ Brasil</a>.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>“O protagonismo dos deficientes visuais na área de tecnologia da informação”</strong> —
			Digital Accessibility track, TDC Porto Alegre, 2017.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>“Como deficientes visuais desenvolvem software — da concepção até uma implementação
			prática”</strong> — Digital Accessibility track, TDC Porto Alegre, 2017.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>podProgramar</strong> — interviewed on
			<a href="https://mundopodcast.com.br/podprogramar/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">one of Brazil's best-known programming podcasts</a>.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>BlindTec</strong> — nearly a decade of articles, tutorials and podcast episodes on
			assistive technology, in my
			<a href="https://www.blindtec.com.br/blog/author/marlonsousa/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">author archive</a>.
		</li>
	</ul>
	<figure class="personal-photo">
		<Image
			src={summit}
			width={640}
			height={427}
			alt="Marlon in a leather armchair in a sunlit loft at Cognition's offices, one hand raised mid-sentence, talking to about eight people seated in a loose circle around him. Tall factory windows and a potted plant stand behind."
		/>
		<figcaption>
			In discussion at the Cognition Champions Summit, San Francisco, June 2026. Photo by Cognition.
		</figcaption>
	</figure>

	<h2>Areas of Interest</h2>
	<p>In the order I currently care about them:</p>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<strong>AI Applied to Software Engineering:</strong> What it takes for an agent to produce
			software professionals can actually depend on — specifications, architecture set up front,
			fast feedback loops, and gates the agent can check itself against. This includes Model Context
			Protocol servers such as <code>screen-readers-mcp</code>, which let agents drive screen readers
			to run automated functional tests, bringing real coverage to software that has historically
			been checked by hand, if at all.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Banking Development:</strong> Core banking, card authorisation, ledgers, and payments —
			correctness, reliability, and trust in systems where being wrong costs somebody real money.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Rust Development:</strong> My language of choice. I am interested in the case that
			Rust is not only for systems work — that you can write command line tools, services, and
			desktop applications in it at a high level of abstraction and still get the performance,
			the deployment story, and the safety guarantees for free.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Open Source Development:</strong> Building in public, mostly assistive technology:
			NVDA add-ons, screen reader ergonomics, and tools the accessibility community can pick up and
			use without asking anyone's permission.
		</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>Education</h2>
	<ul>
		<li><strong>Postgraduate MBA in Mobile &amp; Game Development (2016–2017):</strong> <a href="https://www.fiap.com.br" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FIAP</a></li>
		<li><strong>Technologist in IT Management (2011–2013):</strong> <a href="https://www.unisul.br" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Unisul</a></li>
		<li><strong>Technical Diploma in Data Processing (2002–2003):</strong> Etec Lauro Gomes</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>Explore the Site</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>Read my articles and article series on the <a href="/blog">Blog</a>.</li>
		<li>Discover my open-source projects on the <a href="/projects">Projects</a> page.</li>
		<li>Download the latest add-on builds on the <a href="/releases">Releases</a> page.</li>
		<li>Search all content using the <a href="/search">Search</a> page.</li>
	</ul>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
	/* The portrait sits beside the opening paragraphs on wide screens and above
	   them on narrow ones, so the text column never drops below a readable width. */
	.intro::after {
		content: '';
		display: block;
		clear: both;
	}
	.portrait {
		float: left;
		margin: 0.3rem 1.5rem 0.75rem 0;
		border-radius: 12px;
	}
	/* Below this width the text column beside a 200px portrait gets too narrow
	   to read, so the portrait stops floating and sits above the prose. */
	@media (max-width: 34rem) {
		.portrait {
			float: none;
			margin: 0 0 1rem 0;
		}
	}
	.personal-photo {
		margin: 0 0 1.5rem 0;
	}
	.personal-photo img {
		border-radius: 12px;
	}
	.personal-photo figcaption {
		margin-top: 0.4rem;
		font-size: 0.9em;
		color: rgb(var(--gray));
	}
</style>
